    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/assets/a740aafbb9f74e2681506d3027f848fa92e7eea2bac3e08ccaf3fc7e0acf8fa1.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown in the graph above?",
    "worked_solution": "Consider the line $y=x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
